.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PROC 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm proc
.Nd kernel tables and counters as files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /proc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each file is generated into a buffer when opened.
Every read through that descriptor uses the same snapshot.
A report whose text cannot be allocated is refused with
.Er ENOMEM ,
and
.Pa /proc/disk ,
the one file with a fixed buffer, is refused with
.Er EOVERFLOW
when it fills; a USB record longer than its 96-byte buffer is cut short,
its newline possibly with it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -ohang
.It Pa /proc/audio
The active audio driver and cached HDA enumeration and stream state.
The file exists when audio is disabled; then
.Ql hda ready 0
indicates no selected HDA path.
Opening it sends no codec verb.
.Pp
The codec line identifies the codec address, vendor and audio function group.
Primary and headphone lines give pin, DAC and expanded connection indices;
the primary line also gives its optional mixer or selector.
.Ql enabled
is the pin enable decision from the last setup.
The stream line gives the last successful request and programmed rate,
then controller, primary DAC and headphone DAC format words read back
at setup.
Before any stream these fields are zero.
Stopping clears
.Ql playing
and retains the last setup.
.Pp
Each
.Ql caps dac
line gives the DAC, the node supplying its effective capabilities, and
hexadecimal PCM and stream-format masks.
PCM bits 0 through 11 correspond, in order, to 8000, 11025, 16000, 22050,
32000, 44100, 48000, 88200, 96000, 176400, 192000 and 384000 Hz.
PCM bits 16 and 17 indicate 8-bit and 16-bit samples.
Stream-format bit 0 indicates PCM.
.Pp
The selected pins and mixer have
.Ql connections nid
lines with the long-form flag and packed hexadecimal entries.
Each following
.Ql expanded
line lists NIDs in selector-index order, starting at index zero.
.Ql invalid
marks a rejected list; its raw entries stop where decoding failed.
These are enumeration and setup records, not a measurement of sound.
Use
.Xr audiotest 1
to submit a tone from SSH.
.It Pa /proc/cmdline
The boot command line, followed by a newline.
.It Pa /proc/console
Serial console counters, one
.Ql name value
pair per line:
.Bl -tag -width reader-drains
.It Li uart-bytes , uart-overruns
Bytes received by the UART and UART overruns.
.It Li irq-drains , irq-bytes
Receive drains from the interrupt handler and bytes received there.
.It Li tick-drains , reader-drains
Receive drains from timer ticks and console readers.
.It Li ring-full , ring-max
Full receive ring count and maximum ring occupancy.
.It Li shell-bytes
Bytes taken by the kernel's console polls.
.It Li line-drops , queue-drops
Drops from the input line and input queue.
.It Li flush-bytes , partial-moved , read-bytes
Bytes discarded by a flush, moved from a partial canonical line to
raw input, and read.
.It Li queue-max , line-max
Maximum input queue and line occupancy.
.El
.It Pa /proc/disk
Disk records and counters, described in
.Xr disk 7 .
The initial record has named fields for disk 0, its probe state,
driver policy, identity, size, partitions, and counters.
Before a probe it reports
.Ql state not-asked ;
a probe that finds no disk reports
.Ql state no-device .
.Pp
The registered disks follow in this form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
disk N TRANSPORT LOCATION SECTORS TABLE MODEL
identity N SECTOR-SIZE fixed|removable mounted|free SERIAL
part N M START COUNT ID LABEL
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar N
is the disk number and
.Ar M
the partition number.
.Ar START
and
.Ar COUNT
are sectors.
.Ar TRANSPORT
is
.Ql ahci ,
.Ql ata ,
or
.Ql usb .
.Ar LOCATION
is the AHCI port, ATA position, or USB class-driver slot times four
plus the logical unit number.
ATA positions 0 through 3 are primary master, primary slave,
secondary master, and secondary slave.
.Ar TABLE
is
.Ql mbr ,
.Ql gpt ,
.Ql gpt-bad ,
.Ql none ,
or
.Ql unread .
.Ar ID
is a GPT partition GUID or
.Ql mbr:
followed by the two-digit hexadecimal partition type.
.Ar LABEL
is the GPT name and is empty for MBR.
.Pp
Opening the file issues no disk command.
A report exceeding its fixed buffer bound is refused.
.It Pa /proc/filesystems
One file system type per line.
The two synthetic file systems have the lines
.Ql nodev devfs
and
.Ql nodev proc ,
with a tab between fields.
Each remaining line is a type accepted for mounting after a tab, and
after
.Ql nodev
for a type that stands on no device:
.Ql nodev tmpfs .
.It Pa /proc/interrupts
One line per PIC or MSI vector, numbered 32 through 63, with a nonzero
interrupt count:
.Dl VECTOR: COUNT PIC|MSI NAMES
Fields are separated by tabs.
Shared handlers have comma-separated names; an unnamed vector has
.Ql none .
The final lines are
.Ql SPU: COUNT spurious
and
.Ql FBK: COUNT usb fallback ,
for APIC spurious interrupts and USB timer drains that found work without
an observed pending USB interrupt after any USB interrupt source was enabled.
The USB pump checks each configured controller's MSI vector or PCI IRQ.
A message still in transit can also increase the fallback count.
.It Pa /proc/loop
One line per occupied loop device:
.Dl loopN BYTES rw|ro writes WRITES syncs SYNCS PATH
The fields give its size in bytes, write permission, write and sync
counts, and the first 63 bytes of the path used for attachment, or
.Ql (gone)
for a device whose medium was removed while a mount or a transfer still
holds it.
An unused device has no line.
.It Pa /proc/meminfo
Named memory counts, one
.Ql name: value
pair per line.
.Li MemTotal
and
.Li MemFree
give the total and free physical frame pool.
.Li Buffers
and
.Li Cached
give resident buffer-cache and page-cache memory.
.Li MemAvailable
is free memory plus both caches.
These values have the suffix
.Ql kB
and are in kibibytes.
.Li HeapPages
is the kernel heap's page count;
.Li HeapUsed
is the number of bytes in use within it.
.Li SwapTotal
and
.Li SwapFree
are both
.Ql 0 kB .
.It Pa /proc/mounts
One line per mount, with six whitespace-separated fields:
.Dl SOURCE TARGET TYPE OPTIONS 0 0
The root, the synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
mounts, and the tmpfs at
.Pa /dev/shm
precede mounts made by programs.
The source, target, type, and options describe the mounted file system.
Options are comma-separated:
.Ql ro
or
.Ql rw ,
and the applicable
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev ,
.Ql noexec ,
.Ql noatime ,
.Ql nodiratime ,
and
.Ql relatime
flags.
.It Pa /proc/net/arp
A header followed by one row per complete ARP cache entry.
The columns are IP address, hardware type, flags, hardware address,
mask, and device.
IP addresses are dotted decimal; hardware addresses are six
colon-separated hexadecimal bytes.
Hardware type is
.Ql 0x1 ,
flags are
.Ql 0x2 ,
and the mask is
.Ql * .
The device is the interface on which the mapping was learned.
.It Pa /proc/net/dev
Two header lines followed by one line per network interface.
Each line begins with the interface name and a colon, then sixteen
counters in this order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Receive:  bytes packets errs drop fifo frame compressed multicast
Transmit: bytes packets errs drop fifo colls carrier compressed
.Ed
.Pp
The receive FIFO and both compressed counters are zero.
Receive drops include frames missed by the card.
The frame, multicast, transmit error, FIFO, collision, and carrier
counts come from the card when its driver supplies them; otherwise
they are zero.
.It Pa /proc/net/route
A header followed by each configured interface's subnet route and,
when configured, default route.
The eleven columns are
.Ql Iface Destination Gateway Flags RefCnt Use Metric Mask MTU Window IRTT .
Addresses and masks are eight hexadecimal digits in reversed byte order.
Flags are hexadecimal:
.Ql 0001
for the subnet route and
.Ql 0003
for the default gateway.
Reference count, use, metric, MTU, window, and IRTT are zero.
.It Pa /proc/net/tcp
A header followed by rows for TCP connections, including listeners
and connections still closing.
The kernel counts connections, allocates the snapshot array, then copies
up to the original count.
Connections created during allocation may be included and can displace
older connections from the snapshot.
Each row gives a decimal index followed by a colon, local and remote
addresses, state, transmit and receive queue sizes, timers,
retransmission count, user ID, timeout, and inode.
.Pp
Addresses have the form
.Ql AAAAAAAA:PPPP :
an eight-digit hexadecimal IPv4 address in reversed byte order and a
four-digit hexadecimal port.
State is the two-digit hexadecimal Linux state number.
Queue sizes are paired as
.Ql TTTTTTTT:RRRRRRRR .
The timer pair is
.Ql 00:00000000 ;
the retransmission count has eight hexadecimal digits.
User ID and inode are decimal; timeout is zero.
User ID and inode remain available while the connection is closing.
.Pp
After the inode, each row has the fixed fields
.Ql 1 0000000000000000 100 0 0 10 0
and decimal values with the prefixes
.Ql wnd= ,
.Ql cwnd= ,
.Ql flight= ,
.Ql unsent= ,
.Ql rto= ,
.Ql zwp= ,
.Ql refused= ,
and
.Ql for= .
.It Pa /proc/stat
System counters, one named record per line.
The first two lines have this form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cpu  0 0 BUSY IDLE 0 0 0 0 0 0
cpu0 0 0 BUSY IDLE 0 0 0 0 0 0
.Ed
.Pp
The first four columns are user, nice, system, and idle ticks.
These machine-wide counters do not separate user and system time, which
only the per-process ones in
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
do: busy ticks appear in the system column, and user and nice are zero.
.Pp
The remaining lines have a name and one value:
.Bl -tag -width loopback_delivered_call
.It Li intr
Total interrupt count.
.It Li ctxt
Zero.
.It Li btime
Boot time as seconds since the epoch.
.It Li processes
Processes created since boot.
.It Li sleeps_interrupted
Sleeps in
.Xr nanosleep 2
and
.Xr clock_nanosleep 2
that a signal ended before their deadline, since boot.
A sleep that ran its whole length is not counted.
.It Li latency_timer_us
Longest gap between timer ticks, in microseconds.
.It Li latency_disk_us
Longest block request from acceptance to completion, in microseconds.
.It Li latency_diskwait_us
Longest wait for a block request slot, in microseconds.
.It Li latency_console_us
Longest delay from a console line becoming readable to its being read,
in microseconds.
.It Li loopback_dropped
Datagrams dropped because the loopback ring was full.
.It Li loopback_peak
Maximum number of datagrams in the loopback ring at once.
.It Li loopback_delivered_tick
Datagrams delivered by the timer tick's loopback drain.
.It Li loopback_delivered_idle
Datagrams delivered by the idle wait's loopback drain.
.It Li loopback_delivered_call
Datagrams delivered by the loopback drain at system call exit.
.It Li procs_running , procs_blocked
Current runnable and blocked process counts.
.El
.It Pa /proc/uptime
Seconds since boot and seconds spent idle, separated by a space,
each with two decimal places.
Both derive from delivered 100 Hz timer ticks.
Ticks lost while interrupts are disabled are not counted.
.It Pa /proc/usb
One line per configured USB device:
.Dl port PATH SPEED VENDOR:PRODUCT class CLASS protocol PROTOCOL
.Ar PATH
is the root port followed by any hub ports.
.Ar SPEED
is
.Ql low ,
.Ql full ,
.Ql high ,
.Ql super ,
or
.Ql unknown .
Vendor and product IDs are hexadecimal; class and protocol are decimal.
.Pp
USB disks follow in this form:
.Dl diskN VENDOR PRODUCT SECTORS sectors K parts STATE flushes F
The sector count is 64-bit.
.Ar STATE
is
.Ql reading ,
.Ql ready ,
or
.Ql closing .
A disk not yet registered is named
.Ql disk? .
Write-protected disks append
.Ql ro ;
closing disks append
.Ql holds H .
After a USB disk has been present, a final line gives free disk names
and disks reclaimed since boot:
.Dl disks FREE free RECLAIMED reclaimed
The file is empty when no USB devices are configured and no disk
accounting remains.
.It Pa /proc/PID/stat
One line of 24 space-separated fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
1 pid           2 (comm)         3 state         4 ppid
5 pgrp          6 session        7 tty_nr        8 tpgid
9 flags        10 minflt        11 cminflt      12 majflt
13 cmajflt     14 utime         15 stime        16 cutime
17 cstime      18 priority      19 nice         20 num_threads
21 itrealvalue 22 starttime     23 vsize        24 rss
.Ed
.Pp
The command name is parenthesized; parentheses within it become
underscores.
State is
.Ql R
for running or ready,
.Ql S
for blocked, or
.Ql Z
for zombie.
The parent, process group, and session IDs are recorded values.
Fields 14 through 17 are the user and system time of all the process's
threads and its reaped children's, in 1/100 second ticks, as
.Xr times 2
reports them.
In
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/stat ,
fields 14 and 15 are the thread's own time, as on Linux.
Field 19 is the nice value
.Xr setpriority 2
keeps, and field 18 the priority Linux reports for it, 20 more than the
nice value.
The terminal number is 0, terminal process group is -1, and field 20 is
the number of threads.
Fields 9 through 13, interval timer, start time, virtual size, and
resident size are zero.
.It Pa /proc/PID/cmdline
The arguments as the program's initial stack has them, each ended by a
NUL; a process with no address space shows its program name alone.
.It Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
A symbolic link for each open descriptor N, whose target names what the
descriptor is open on: a file of the KRFFS root by its path now, a file
of another type by the path it was opened by, a device by its
.Pa /dev
path, a pipe or a FIFO as
.Ql pipe:[INODE] ,
a socket as
.Ql socket:[INODE] ,
an eventfd, epoll or timerfd descriptor as
.Ql anon_inode: Ns Ar NAME ,
and a file of
.Xr memfd_create 2
as
.Ql /memfd: Ns Ar NAME Ns Ql " (deleted)" ,
as Linux names each.
A followed link reports the descriptor's attributes.
A descriptor that is closed has no link, also while its release waits
for a call of another thread that is using it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/exe
A symbolic link to the program the process runs, by the path its
.Xr execve 2
found it at.
A path through it reaches the program, so an
.Xr open 2 ,
a
.Xr stat 2
or an
.Xr execve 2
of
.Pa /proc/self/exe
is one of the program itself, as on Linux, where a program runs itself
again this way.
Reading the link needs effective user ID 0 or the process's real user
ID.
.It Pa /proc/PID/task
A directory with an entry for each thread of the process, named by its
thread ID, the thread whose ID is the process ID first.
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID
names nothing unless TID is one of the threads the directory lists, and
has the files of
.Pa /proc/TID ,
which are the thread's own where a thread has any, and no
.Pa task
directory of its own.
A descriptor open on it, or on its
.Pa fd ,
keeps both IDs: its link names that path, and a name relative to it is
found under it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/comm
The program name and a newline.
.It Pa /proc/PID/maps
A line for each region of the address space:
.Dl start-end perms offset 00:00 0 path
with the rights as
.Ql r ,
.Ql w ,
.Ql x
or
.Ql - ,
then
.Ql p
for a private region or
.Ql s
for a shared one, and from column 73 the file the region maps,
.Ql [heap] ,
.Ql [stack] ,
or the program for the segments of its image.
The device and inode are not kept and read 0.
.It Pa /proc/PID/status
Lines of a name, a colon, a tab and a value:
Name, Umask, State, Tgid, Pid, PPid, TracerPid, Uid and Gid (real,
effective, saved and file-system IDs), FDSize, Groups, the memory sizes
VmPeak to VmSwap in kilobytes, Threads, the signal sets SigPnd, ShdPnd,
SigBlk, SigIgn and SigCgt in hexadecimal, the capability sets, full for
effective user ID 0 and empty otherwise, and NoNewPrivs.
.It Pa /proc/PID/statm
Seven sizes in pages: total, resident, shared, text, library, data with
stack, and dirty; the third, fifth and seventh read 0.
.It Pa /proc/PID/auxv
The auxiliary vector the program was given, as pairs of 32-bit words
through the
.Dv AT_NULL
pair.
.It Pa /proc/PID/environ
The environment strings of the initial stack, each ended by a NUL.
It,
.Pa auxv
and the
.Pa exe
link need effective user ID 0 or the process's real user ID.
.It Pa /proc/PID/mem
The process's memory, read and written at the descriptor's offset, which
is the address, as GDB uses it through
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2 .
An address no region allows is
.Er EIO ,
and a write reaches read-only program text, as a tracer's does.
Opening it needs effective user ID 0, or the process's real user ID and a
dumpable process.
.It Pa /proc/self
The calling thread's process.
.Pa /proc
lists each process once; the ID of a thread that did not begin its
process also names a directory, which is not listed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr memory 7 ,
.Xr rach2 7
